---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

import LedGpioCircuitDiagram from "../../../../assets/courses/foce/img/led-gpio-circuit-diagram.svg";
import RedBoardPinCard from "../../../../assets/courses/foce/img/redboard-rp2350-pin-card.svg";
import RedBoardPins28To33 from "../../../../assets/courses/foce/img/redboard-rp2350-pins-28-33.svg";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `from machine import Pin

led = Pin(28, Pin.OUT)
led.value(1)`;
const Task1 = `from machine import Pin

led = Pin(28, Pin.OUT)
led.value(1)
`;
const Task2 = `from machine import Pin
import time

led = Pin(28, Pin.OUT)
led.value(1)
time.sleep(1)
led.value(0)
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.3: Ohm's Law and Meet the Microcontroller</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Electronics Primer &mdash; Day 3 of 3</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 2.3</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Ohm's Law and Meet the Microcontroller</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Ohm's Law</p>
              <p>GPIO Pins</p>
              <p>Objects &amp; Pin()</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Yesterday's Circuit, One Question</h2>
              <p>
                Why <b class="text-sn-theme">330&Omega;</b>, specifically?
                Would a different resistor have worked? Today's math
                answers that.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Ohm's Law</h2>
              <p>
                Voltage, current, and resistance aren't independent &mdash;
                they're locked together by one equation:
              </p>
              <p class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">V = I &times; R</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Voltage (volts) = Current (amps) &times; Resistance (ohms)</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Rearranging the Formula</h2>
              <p>Same equation, solved for a different variable:</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>I = V &divide; R</b> &mdash; solve for current</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>R = V &divide; I</b> &mdash; solve for resistance</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Worked Example: Checking Yesterday's Resistor</h2>
              <p>
                3.3V source, 330&Omega; resistor. How much current flows?
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">I = V &divide; R = 3.3 &divide; 330 = <b class="text-sn-theme">0.01A (10mA)</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                That's a safe, small current for an LED &mdash; which is
                exactly why 330&Omega; gets picked so often, not because
                it's the only option.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Meet the RedBoard RP2350</h2>
              <Image src={RedBoardPins28To33} alt="The SparkFun IoT RedBoard RP2350 with the block of six pins labeled 33 through 28 outlined on the top header" width={620} />
              <p>
                The board you'll use the rest of this unit. Its{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">GPIO pins</b> (General Purpose
                Input/Output) can be controlled entirely by code. The six
                outlined above are the ones you'll wire to.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Digital Pins: On or Off</h2>
              <p>
                A digital pin has exactly two states: <b>HIGH</b>
                (on, about 3.3V) or <b>LOW</b> (off, 0V). No in-between
                &mdash; that comes later, with analog pins.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Meet Your First Object</h2>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="python" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">
                <code>Pin(28, Pin.OUT)</code> creates an{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">object</b> &mdash; something that
                bundles together information (which pin, which direction)
                and actions you can perform on it.{" "}
                <code>led.value(1)</code> calls one of those actions,
                called a <b class="text-sn-theme">method</b>, using a dot.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Calculating voltage, current, or resistance with Ohm's Law</li>
                <li>Explaining what a GPIO pin is and its two digital states</li>
                <li>Writing your first line of code to control a real LED</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Ohm's Law</dt>
                  <dd>The relationship V = I &times; R between voltage, current, and resistance.</dd>
                  <dt>GPIO</dt>
                  <dd>General Purpose Input/Output &mdash; a microcontroller pin that code can control.</dd>
                  <dt>Digital Pin</dt>
                  <dd>A pin with exactly two states: HIGH (on) or LOW (off).</dd>
                  <dt>Object</dt>
                  <dd>Something that bundles data and actions together &mdash; like a Pin, which knows its number and direction, and has actions you can call on it.</dd>
                  <dt>Method</dt>
                  <dd>An action you call on an object using dot notation, like <code>led.value(1)</code>.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Ohm's Law Practice</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A circuit has 3.3V across a 330&Omega; resistor. What's the current?</li>
            <li>A circuit has 3.3V, and you want exactly 3.3mA (0.0033A) of current. What resistance do you need?</li>
            <li>A circuit has a 220&Omega; resistor with 10mA (0.01A) flowing through it. What's the voltage?</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Rewire to a GPIO Pin</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Move your LED and resistor from yesterday's fixed 3.3V pin to GPIO 28, keeping GND the same.</li>
            <li>The LED should now be off &mdash; a GPIO pin starts LOW until code says otherwise.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>Finding GPIO 28 on the board:</b> look along the block of eight
            pins on the top header for the one marked <code>28</code> &mdash;
            its neighbors are <code>29</code> on one side and{" "}
            <code>0/TXD</code> on the other. Keep this card handy; you will be
            hunting for pins all unit.
          </p>

          <Image src={RedBoardPinCard} alt="Reference card for the SparkFun IoT RedBoard RP2350, with the six pins labeled 33 through 28 outlined on the top header, and a note that the printed numbers are GPIO numbers rather than positions" width={620} class="my-3" />

          <Image src={LedGpioCircuitDiagram} alt="Circuit diagram showing the resistor and LED now wired to GPIO 28 instead of the fixed 3.3V pin" width={560} class="my-3" />

          <!-- PHOTO: circuit rewired to GPIO 28, LED off -->
          <div
            class="border border-secondary rounded p-4 my-3 text-center text-secondary"
            style="border-style: dashed !important;"
          >
            [PHOTO PLACEHOLDER &mdash; Task 2: Circuit Rewired to GPIO 28, LED Off]
          </div>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Your First Code</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Create a new MicroPython script named <b class="text-sn-theme">2-3-first-blink.py</b>.</li>
            <li>Import <code>Pin</code>, create a <code>Pin</code> object for GPIO 28 set as output, and turn it on.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="python" title="2-3-first-blink.py" mark={{ range: "4" }} />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: Turn It Off (and Blink Once)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Import <code>time</code>, wait one second after turning the LED on, then turn it off.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="python" title="2-3-first-blink.py" mark={{ range: "6-7" }} ins={{ range: "2,6-7" }} />

          <!-- PHOTO: LED lit while the script runs -->
          <div
            class="border border-secondary rounded p-4 my-3 text-center text-secondary"
            style="border-style: dashed !important;"
          >
            [PHOTO PLACEHOLDER &mdash; Task 3/4: LED Lit Under Code Control]
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>When you run your script, the LED should:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Turn on immediately</li>
            <li>Stay on for about one second</li>
            <li>Turn off, and the script should end</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Walk through the math: given a 3.3V source and a 330&Omega; resistor, what's the current, and how did you calculate it?</li>
            <li>In <code>led = Pin(28, Pin.OUT)</code>, what is <code>led</code>, and what does calling <code>led.value(1)</code> actually do?</li>
            <li>Why did you move the LED from the 3.3V/GND rail to a GPIO pin before writing any code &mdash; what did that change make possible?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
